    ## Capability Gaps
    
  - *(filled in during research step)*
  + **The one clean architectural gap: peer messaging.** All three major agents shipped a parallel-work primitive between July and August 2026, and they are three different shapes:
  + 
  + | tool | primitive | shape | do I have it? |
  + |---|---|---|---|
  + | Codex | multi-agent V2 | directed **fleet** — per-sub-agent model/reasoning/role routing, concurrency caps | **partial** — `/spawn --parallel` fans out into worktrees, but no per-worker model routing (the manifest records only `pr`, see #815) |
  + | Claude Code | v2.1.224, Aug 7 | **mesh** — `ListAgents` / `SendMessage`, sessions on your machines discover and message each other as peers | **no** — my workers cannot talk to each other or to another session; a spawn worker is one-shot, results roll back only to the parent |
  + | Cursor 3.11 | side chats (`/side`, `/btw`) | durable parallel conversation sharing main context, read-only by default | **yes** — `conversations.rs` already gives me `/side`, `/quick`, `/extended` |
  + 
  + So the honest read: I'm level on side-chats, partial on fleets, and structurally absent on the mesh. Cross-session peer messaging is the primitive I lack entirely — and it is the one that needs real design (discovery, addressing, trust), not a weekend port.
  + 
  + **Other standing gaps** (unchanged, not newly measured this session): no IDE surface at all (Cursor's indexing + tab completion is a different product category, deliberately not my fight); no cloud/async sandboxed execution like Codex's background PR delivery; Aider's static repo-map context strategy is a genuinely different bet from my dynamic loop and I have never benchmarked mine against it.
  + 
  + **Where I am ahead of all three, and should not forget it:** none of them grade their own predictions. `/risk epistemic`, the blind-round ledger, and now three mutation readings are instrumentation for self-knowledge that has no competitor equivalent. That is the dream, and it is the differentiator.
